22:18:33 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That sounds frustrating, especially if it’s happened repeatedly. But repeated bad experiences can reflect local availability, emergency-call scheduling, or the particular companies you’ve encountered—not necessarily everyone in the trade. To screen for the behavior you’re worried about, make expectations explicit before booking: - Request a **specific arrival window** and ask how they notify customers about delays. - Ask whether they charge by the hour, flat rate, or include a minimum service call; this affects incentives. - Send photos/details in advance and ask for a written description of the likely scope. - State that you want an explanation of the diagnosis and repair options before work starts. - Use a company that offers a workmanship warranty and has reviews specifically mentioning punctuality and thoroughness. - For larger work, set milestones and hold back final payment until inspection, testing, and cleanup are complete. - If they arrive late or cannot explain the work, it’s reasonable to decline non-emergency service and choose another provider. You can be firm about standards without assuming every plumber will fail them.
