22:18:51 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Stereotypes can grow from recognizable frustrations, but jokes and anecdotes aren’t reliable evidence that an entire occupation behaves the same way. Plumbing work also has real variability: hidden damage, parts runs, permit requirements, and emergency calls can make jobs seem slow or costly. You can protect yourself from overcharging or unnecessary delay with process: - Get a **fixed-price quote** where feasible, with a clear scope and exclusions. - Require approval for any work beyond an agreed threshold. - Ask for **itemized labor, materials, and service-call charges**. - Compare at least two estimates for non-emergency work. - Agree on a start date, expected duration, and communication if delays arise. - Pay by completed milestones for larger projects; don’t pay everything in advance. - Keep photos, messages, invoices, and warranty terms. - If the diagnosis or price seems questionable, get a second opinion before authorizing major work. That addresses the risk without treating individual plumbers as interchangeable.
