22:19:04 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Hourly billing can create an incentive to be careful about how time is used, so it’s reasonable to want safeguards. But it doesn’t mean a plumber has “zero incentive” to work efficiently: reputable businesses depend on repeat customers, reviews, fitting more jobs into the day, avoiding callbacks, and maintaining their reputation. For non-emergency work, reduce the risk with clear terms: - Ask for an **estimated labor-time range** before authorizing work. - Request **itemized invoices** showing labor hours, parts, and any trip or diagnostic fees. - Agree that they must get your approval before exceeding the estimate by, say, 10–20%. - Ask whether they offer **flat-rate pricing** for the specific repair. - Have them explain the diagnosis, proposed repair, and any alternatives before starting. - For major work, get competing quotes and use a written scope with milestones. - Check reviews for concrete comments about billing transparency—not just star ratings. If an hourly plumber is vague about rates, won’t estimate the job, or resists documenting added work, that’s a legitimate reason to choose someone else.
